format 1
adapter safety 1
section: basics

card safety-time-distance-shielding
title: Time, distance, shielding
icon: Timer1
entry: shielding
references: ref-epa-protecting-yourself
summary: Three ways to lower the dose from a radiation source.
steps: Time: spending less time near a source reduces the dose from it.
steps: Distance: the dose decreases dramatically as you move farther from the source.
steps: Shielding: a proper barrier between you and a source greatly reduces or eliminates the dose.
steps: Lead, concrete or water barriers protect from penetrating gamma rays.

card safety-background-radiation
title: Background radiation
icon: Sun1
entry: background-radiation
references: ref-nrc-doses-daily-lives
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation
summary: Radiation has always been part of the natural environment.
steps: Radiation has always been all around us as part of our natural environment.
steps: Most background exposure comes from radon in the air. Cosmic rays and the Earth add smaller shares.
steps: Food and water carry some natural radioactive material, such as potassium-40.
steps: Medical procedures account for nearly all human exposure to man-made radiation.

card safety-what-is-a-dose
title: What a dose is
icon: Chart
entry: absorbed-dose
references: ref-epa-radiation-health-effects
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation
summary: How much radiation the body takes in, and how fast, both matter.
steps: Damage depends on the absorbed dose, measured in grays. The effective dose is in sieverts.
steps: The dose rate matters too: it is how fast the dose is delivered.
steps: Effects such as burns are more severe at higher doses and higher dose rates.
steps: Low doses delivered slowly carry a lower risk, because the body is more likely to repair the damage.

card safety-exposure-vs-contamination
title: Exposure or contamination?
icon: Drop
entry: exposure
references: ref-cdc-contamination-vs-exposure
references: ref-hps-radioactive-contamination
summary: Being exposed to radiation is not the same as having radioactive material on you.
steps: Exposure is radiation reaching a person, like the beam of an x-ray.
steps: Contamination is radioactive material on or inside the body.
steps: A person exposed to radiation is not necessarily contaminated.
steps: Radiation does not spread or get on people. Radioactive contamination is what can be spread.
